WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.877
VectorMapNet is the first end-to-end pipeline that
reads onboard camera and LiDAR and

00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:08.060
predicts a sparse set of polylines

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.371
the primitive planners actually use.

00:00:10.451 --> 00:00:14.480
It skips rasterization entirely and sets a new
state of the art.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.663
Self-driving cars need HD maps marking lanes,
boundaries, and crosswalks.

00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:24.345
Today these are annotated by hand, which is costly
and does not scale.

00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:27.824
Learning methods instead predict a dense pixel grid,

00:00:27.904 --> 00:00:32.640
but it carries no individual elements and needs
brittle post-processing.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.580
The authors argue mapping should happen online,
from the car's own sensors,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.416
and the model should output the final
representation directly.

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:45.437
Prior methods rasterize then vectorize with a
hand-designed step;

00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:49.520
VectorMapNet removes that detour and predicts
geometry end to end.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.490
VectorMapNet makes three contributions.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:56.118
It predicts vectorized outputs directly from sensors,

00:00:56.198 --> 00:00:58.925
with no rasterization or post-processing.

00:00:59.005 --> 00:01:04.676
It uses the polyline as one flexible primitive
whose vertex order encodes direction.

00:01:04.756 --> 00:01:09.810
And it adapts detection transformers to locate
elements in bird's-eye view.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.111
VectorMapNet has three stages.

00:01:12.191 --> 00:01:18.846
A bird's-eye-view extractor maps each modality into
a shared space: cameras through a ResNet and IPM,

00:01:18.926 --> 00:01:22.247
LiDAR through PointPillars, fused by concatenation.

00:01:22.327 --> 00:01:27.515
A detection transformer with element queries
locates each element as keypoints.

00:01:27.595 --> 00:01:31.849
An autoregressive polyline generator then emits
ordered vertices,

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:34.850
trained with detection and generation losses.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.930
VectorMapNet is evaluated on nuScenes and Argoverse2.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:44.922
Following HDMapNet, predicted polylines are
compared to ground truth for crosswalks,

00:01:45.002 --> 00:01:47.418
lane dividers, and road boundaries.

00:01:47.498 --> 00:01:53.695
It reports Chamfer-distance AP and a new
Fréchet-distance AP that respects vertex order,

00:01:53.775 --> 00:01:55.050
plus 3D evaluation.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.923
VectorMapNet sets a new state of the art.

00:01:59.003 --> 00:02:04.587
It beats HDMapNet by 14.2 mAP on nuScenes and 14.6
on Argoverse2.

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:10.512
Gains hold across sensors: nearly eighteen points
camera, ten LiDAR,

00:02:10.592 --> 00:02:12.342
over fourteen fusion.

00:02:12.422 --> 00:02:17.570
Two-stage fine-tuning pushes fusion to 53.7 mAP
versus 31.0.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.402
Two ablations stand out.

00:02:19.482 --> 00:02:24.830
Representing each element by a bounding box with
two keypoints beats the alternatives

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.703
by two Fréchet and over seven Chamfer points.

00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:32.556
Two-stage training, teacher forcing then
fine-tuning on predicted keypoints,

00:02:32.636 --> 00:02:35.940
adds about seven mAP for camera and eight for fusion.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.922
To summarize: VectorMapNet exceeds the previous
state of the art by 14.2 mAP on nuScenes and 14.6 on Argoverse2.

00:02:42.002 --> 00:02:47.605
Its best configuration, fusion with fine-tuning,

00:02:47.685 --> 00:02:53.287
reaches 53.7 mAP where HDMapNet reaches 31.

00:02:53.368 --> 00:02:58.970
Gains span sensors: about eighteen for camera, ten
for LiDAR, fourteen for fusion.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:03.618
The takeaway: HD mapping needs no
rasterize-then-vectorize detour.

00:03:03.698 --> 00:03:08.230
Treating mapping as detection plus autoregressive
polyline generation,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.117
VectorMapNet produces directional vector maps from
sensors,

00:03:12.197 --> 00:03:16.860
beats rasterized pipelines by double digits, and
extends to centerlines.
